# ============================================================================
# WHERE EACH KIND OF VALUE GOES — read this before copying anything below.
#
# A .env file is BUILD-TIME and effectively PUBLIC. Next loads .env, .env.local
# and .env.production while it builds, and @opennextjs/cloudflare copies
# everything it loaded into the Worker bundle that gets uploaded to Cloudflare.
# A secret in .env.local is therefore a secret published inside the site's own
# code. That happened on 2026-09-02; see .agents/DEPLOY.md.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_* .............. .env.production   (build-time, inlined, public)
#   runtime secret, locally .... .dev.vars         (gitignored, never bundled)
#   runtime secret, production . wrangler secret put <NAME>
#   script-only credential ..... your shell, or .dev.vars
#
# `pnpm deploy` runs scripts/check-build-env.mjs and ABORTS if the bundle carries
# anything that is not NEXT_PUBLIC_*. This file is a catalogue of names, not a
# file to copy wholesale to .env.local.
#
# Note: `pnpm dev` (plain next dev) does not read .dev.vars, so quote delivery is
# inert there — that is correct. Use `pnpm preview`, which runs the real workerd
# runtime and does read it.
# ============================================================================

# Public base URL of the deployed site (no trailing slash).
# Used for metadataBase, canonical URLs, hreflang alternates, sitemap and robots.
# The ONLY variable that belongs in a .env file. Production: .env.production.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# --- Cloudflare Stream (private, signed-URL video playback) ---
# NOT ONE OF THESE BELONGS IN A .env FILE. They are listed here as a catalogue of
# names; every one of them lives in .dev.vars locally and, where the Worker needs
# it, in `wrangler secret put` for production. `pnpm video:stream` reads and
# writes .dev.vars and never touches .env.local.
#
# Full walkthrough, written for a non-developer: .agents/video-setup.md

# --- Local CLI only ----------------------------------------------------------
# These two authenticate YOUR MACHINE to Cloudflare's management API so
# `pnpm video:stream` can mint signing keys and lock videos. The deployed Worker
# never needs them, so never `wrangler secret put` them: an unused credential on
# the server is pure downside.

# Cloudflare account that owns the Stream videos. An identifier, not a secret —
# it is printed on every page of the dashboard, and it is recorded in
# .agents/infra.md. scripts/stream.mjs already defaults to it, so leave it unset
# unless you are pointing the scripts at a different account.
CF_ACCOUNT_ID=""

# API token with Stream:Edit + Account Settings:Read, created at
# dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens. A SECRET. Put it in .dev.vars.
CF_STREAM_API_TOKEN=""

# --- Worker runtime, required for playback -----------------------------------
# The two variables below are the only ones the deployed Worker actually needs.
# .dev.vars locally; `wrangler secret put` in production.

# ID of the Stream signing key used to mint short-lived playback JWTs.
# Written to .dev.vars by `pnpm video:stream keys`.
CF_STREAM_SIGNING_KEY_ID=""

# Private key paired with CF_STREAM_SIGNING_KEY_ID. Accepts either the base64
# blob Cloudflare's API returns in `pem`, or a literal PKCS#8 PEM block.
# A SECRET, and Cloudflare shows its private half exactly once.
CF_STREAM_SIGNING_KEY_PEM=""

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Per-account delivery host, e.g. "customer-a1b2c3d4e5f6.cloudflarestream.com".
# Find it with `pnpm video:stream subdomain`. Defaults to videodelivery.net,
# which also works but is the legacy form. Not a secret, but still runtime
# configuration: .dev.vars and `wrangler secret put`, not a .env file.
CF_STREAM_CUSTOMER_SUBDOMAIN=""

# How long a minted playback URL stays valid, in seconds. Default 7200 (2 hours).
# Cloudflare caps this at 86400 (24 hours) and so do we. Shorter is safer: it is
# the window in which a URL copied out of someone's browser still plays.
CF_STREAM_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS="7200"

# Extra private video links as a JSON array, in the same shape as the entries in
# src/lib/video/links.ts. Lets a link be added or revoked with
# `wrangler secret put PRIVATE_VIDEO_LINKS` instead of a code change. Entries
# here override entries in links.ts with the same token.
# NOTE: this contains secret tokens. It must never be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# Because it is a secret rather than a public file, an entry here may state the
# token in plaintext. src/lib/video/links.ts may NOT: that file is on GitHub, so
# it stores `tokenHash` (base64url SHA-256 of the token) and the site refuses any
# file entry that ships a plaintext token. Either form works here.
# Example: [{"token":"<32-chars>","clips":[{"uid":"<32-hex-chars>"}]}]
#      or: [{"tokenHash":"<43-chars>","clips":[{"uid":"<32-hex-chars>"}]}]
PRIVATE_VIDEO_LINKS=""

# --- Quote form delivery (server-only) ---------------------------------------
# The quote form on /contact and the home page is delivered through a small
# provider interface (src/components/forms/quote/delivery.ts). Two providers
# ship: Telegram and Resend. Adding a third is a new object in that file.
#
# Precedence is Telegram, then Resend. If both are configured Telegram wins and
# no email is sent. Deleting the Telegram secrets is how you fall back to email.
#
# With all of these unset the form still validates and still tells the visitor
# the truth: it shows the "could not be sent" panel with the phone number and
# WhatsApp link, and writes the full submission to the server log prefixed
# `[quote] UNDELIVERED` so nothing is lost. It never shows a false success.

# --- Telegram (the channel the client actually uses) -------------------------
# Full walkthrough, written for a non-developer: .agents/telegram-setup.md
#
# Both are required together; one without the other counts as unconfigured.
# Both are RUNTIME values: .dev.vars locally, `wrangler secret put` in production.
# Live since 2026-09-02 — bot @TopCleaningMD_Bot, chat id 5127988710.

# From @BotFather after /newbot. A SECRET: never prefix this NEXT_PUBLIC_, never
# paste it into a chat or a command line.
# In production: `wrangler secret put TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN`.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=""

# The numeric id of the chat the bot sends quote requests to. NOT a phone
# number — Telegram cannot address a bot message by phone number. Get it with
# `pnpm telegram:chat-id` after sending /start to the bot from the account that
# should receive quotes.
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=""

# --- Resend (email) — OPTIONAL, AND NOT SET UP -------------------------------
# Nothing below is required. Telegram above is the live delivery path and it is
# configured; Resend is the fallback the code keeps so that switching to email is
# one `wrangler secret put` away. It has never had an API key, in production or
# anywhere else, and the form does not need one. Set these ONLY if you have
# decided to move quote requests off Telegram and onto email — and remember that
# Telegram takes precedence, so you must also delete the two Telegram secrets or
# nothing will change.

# Resend API key (https://resend.com → API Keys). Server-only: never prefix
# this NEXT_PUBLIC_. In production use `wrangler secret put RESEND_API_KEY`.
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# Where quote requests are emailed. The client's own inbox. Read ONLY by the
# Resend provider — it is not a Telegram setting and it does not affect the live
# form. (It is nonetheless set as a Worker secret in production, left over from
# before the switch; harmless, and it becomes correct again if email is ever
# turned on.)
QUOTE_NOTIFY_EMAIL=""

# Optional. The From address, which must be on a domain verified in Resend.
# Defaults to Resend's shared "onboarding@resend.dev" sender, which works with
# no DNS setup but only delivers to the address that owns the Resend account —
# fine for a first deploy, not for production.
QUOTE_FROM_EMAIL=""
